<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
  <Content>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. HIGHWAY 2 - SONORAN DESERT - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Heat ripples off two-lane blacktop. A dusty COMPACT CAR pushes north past dead cotton fields. On the passenger seat: a black funeral dress, still in its dry-cleaning bag.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Behind the wheel, MARISOL REYES (38), city clothes, desert eyes. She turns the radio off and drives in silence.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A road sign staggers past: SAN BLAS 12. Beneath the town name, someone has spray-painted two words in dripping white: SIN AGUA.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She passes a line of parked farm trucks on the shoulder. Men stand in the thin shade of their own flatbeds, watching her city plates go by. Nobody waves.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Far off, where the river used to run, a heron lifts out of the dry bed and beats its way north. Marisol watches it go until the road curves.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. REYES FAMILY HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A wake in a small kitchen. Mourners murmur in the living room beyond. Marisol, alone, reaches into the flour tin above the stove — her father’s old hiding place — and pulls out a LEATHER LEDGER, flour-white at the edges.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to herself)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You never stopped keeping score, Papá.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The kitchen door swings. Marisol slides the ledger into her purse in one practiced motion. TÍA CARMEN (70s), small and iron-spined, sets down a tower of borrowed folding chairs.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CARMEN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your uncle is telling the governor story again. Third time. Each time the governor gets taller.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And Papá gets smaller.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Through the doorway: RAÚL REYES (60) holds court among the mourners, linen shirt, easy laughter. He catches Marisol’s eye and raises his coffee to her like a toast.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CAMEN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(low, not looking at her)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Whatever your father hid from that man, mija — decide fast what you are going to do with it. Grief makes the house easy to search.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SAN BLAS CEMETERY - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fresh earth. Marisol stands beside LUPITA (16), her niece, all elbows and eyeliner. The crowd thins around them.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Everybody keeps saying he died of a tired heart. Nadie se muere de eso, tía. Nobody just gets tired.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Some men do. This town helps.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Beyond the low wall, a rented backhoe waits with its engine running. The operator checks his phone. The mourners drift toward their trucks.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Is it true you audit narcos in the city?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Banks. Which is narcos with better lawyers.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Raúl arrives, sets a warm hand on Marisol’s shoulder.  She doesn’t turn.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Come by the office tomorrow, mija. Family should not talk business over a grave.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He goes. Lupita watches Marisol watch him. The backhoe drops into gear.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. AGUAS DEL VALLE - RAÚL’S OFFICE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A water company office pretending to be modest. RAÚL REYES (60), Marisol’s uncle, linen shirt, slides a folder across the desk. A check clipped to the front.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your father’s share of the concession. I’m offering double what it’s worth, mija. Sign it, go back to your life.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(not touching the folder)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Double. For dirt with no water under it?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Raúl leans back. On the wall behind him: sixty years of framed photographs. Canals full. Cotton high. Men shaking hands.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This valley was dying before you were born. Your grandfather knew it. Your father pretended not to. Somebody had to keep the water moving where it still grows something. That somebody eats last and gets blamed first. I have kept this family fed for forty years.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then the books will show exactly that, tío. Feeding.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A long moment. The air conditioner hums like a held breath. Raúl smiles, unclips the check, and puts it in his shirt pocket.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your father also thought he was an accountant first and a Reyes second. Ask Carmen how that ended.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. EL CAMINO MOTEL - ROOM 7 - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ledger pages spread across a sagging bed. Marisol works a calculator,  sticky notes climbing the headboard like ivy. She circles one column twice.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(quiet)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty thousand cubic meters. Every dry season. Where are you going?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She tapes ledger pages to the mirror in a grid, connects entries with eyeliner pencil. A pattern forms: the same initials beside the same canal, year after year. EL P.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into phone)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I need two more weeks. Unpaid, fine. Family matter. No — a numbers matter. With my family attached.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She hangs up. Looks at the mirror: her own face behind the taped arithmetic, divided into columns. She circles one entry so hard the pencil snaps. CANAL SIETE — 40,000 M3 — EL PATRÓN.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. CANAL SIETE - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A concrete canal, bone dry. FARMERS wait beside empty pipes. CHUY ORTEGA (50s), Aguas del Valle foreman, padlocks the head gate under their stares.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHUY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Allotments changed. Take it up with the office.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I am the office now, Chuy. Twelve percent of it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The oldest farmer, DON ESTEBAN (80), doesn’t raise his voice. He doesn’t have to. The others go still to hear him.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DON ESTEBAN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Your father walked this canal every Friday of his life, señora. He wrote in his little book and the water came. He stopped walking in March. You do the mathematics.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who changed the allotments, Chuy? A name.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHUY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(clicking the padlock shut)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Paper changes. Water obeys. That is all I know since March.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He walks away down the dry bed, boots loud on the cracked concrete. The farmers watch Marisol now. All of them.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. EJIDO SAN BLAS - WELL FIELD - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A municipal water tanker, mobbed politely. Families queue with every container they own: drums, buckets, a pink piñata bucket. A MUNICIPAL DEPUTY manages the line with a clipboard he never writes on.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Fifty meters off, a drilling rig grinds into the earth and coughs up nothing but pale dust. Lupita, phone out, films the rig, the line, the deputy.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEPUTY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No filming, señorita. Municipal operation.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It’s a public well. On public land. With public nothing coming out of it.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Marisol arrives on foot, takes in the line, the rig, her niece already in the deputy’s face. The WELL FOREMAN (40s), dust to the eyebrows, kills the rig motor.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>How deep are you?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>WELL FOREMAN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ninety meters. Two years ago this field hit water at sixty. The aquifer is not dropping, señora. It is being spent. Somewhere it is coming out of a pipe with pressure behind it. Not here.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Down the line, the piñata bucket reaches the tanker. The kid holding it gets it half filled. The deputy finally writes something: the time.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. MUNICIPAL ARCHIVE - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Rolling shelves of yellowed folios. A CLERK unrolls the 1962 concession map. Marisol traces the canal lines — and stops at a ragged edge where a page has been razored out.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Who checked this volume out last?</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CLERK</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(already regretting it)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Señora... I like this job.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She waits. The clerk glances at the door, then rolls the map tighter, voice dropping under the ceiling fan.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CLERK</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>The volume was whole in March. I know because the survey team for the new bottling permit used it. Ask instead who commissioned that survey. Ask what the permit annexes say.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He slides a photocopy across the desk, stamped: AGUAS DEL VALLE — SOLICITUD — ANEXO B. Marisol reads. Her jaw sets.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Thank you. This job — you should keep liking it. Loudly. Where people can hear you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. MUNICIPAL ARCHIVE - DAY (LATER THAT WEEK)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A new padlock on the archive door. The Clerk stands outside it with a cardboard box of his things: a mug, a desk fan, a framed diploma. A bus idles at the corner.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CLERK</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Transferred. Hermosillo. Effective yesterday, which is a very fast kind of paperwork for this municipio.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I’m sorry. I did this.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CLERK</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(handing her the desk fan)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Don’t be sorry. Be fast. Anexo B has a twin brother. Anexo C. Volumes, buyer, price per cubic meter. It never touched this archive. He keeps it where he keeps the check he almost gave you.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He boards the bus with his box. It pulls away past the town fountain — dry, of course. Marisol stands holding a stranger’s desk fan in the heat.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. REYES FAMILY HOUSE - PORCH - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Crickets. Marisol and Lupita share a blanket and a bag of chamoy peanuts.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>When I stay at Ceci’s I hear trucks on the old ranch road. Two, three in the morning. Water trucks, tía. In a drought.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(a long beat)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You never told anyone that.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You never asked.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Headlights sweep the porch — a truck passing on the far road, big engine under load. They both track it until the dark takes it back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>That’s the third one tonight. I count them like sheep now.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Marisol checks her watch: 2:41 AM. She takes Lupita’s eyeliner from the peanut bag and writes a plate number on the inside of her own wrist.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. EL CAMINO MOTEL - ROOM 7 - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The door hangs open. The room is gutted — mattress flipped, sticky notes gone, ledger pages fanned across the floor like shot birds. Headlights sweep the window. Raúl’s pickup idles outside, then rolls away, unhurried.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(into the dark)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Okay, tío. Now I know it’s real.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She rights the mattress. Under it, cracked underfoot by whoever searched: a small framed photo of her father at the canal gate, forty years younger, laughing.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Her hands stop shaking. They start folding, stacking, packing instead — fast and neat, an auditor closing a site. The ledger pages go back in order. All but one. She holds up a page the searchers stepped on and missed: the eyeliner grid from the mirror, folded small.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. SAN BLAS - PLAZA PRINCIPAL - DAY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Banners across the kiosk: AGUAS DEL VALLE — 60 AÑOS SIRVIENDO A SAN BLAS. The plaza loudspeakers crackle with a live radio remote. Raúl’s voice pours out warm as syrup over the dry fountain.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(over speakers)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>This Friday, the Reyes family answers the drought the way we always have — with works, not words. A community water trust. Free tanker service for every colonia, twice a week, starting now, paid from my own pocket.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Scattered applause from the shade. In a parked car at the plaza’s edge, Marisol listens, knuckles white on the wheel.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(over speakers)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>And if you hear wild stories these days — forgive them. My brother’s daughter came home to bury her father. Grief does strange arithmetic, friends. In this family we answer it with love. And with water.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Laughter, applause. Marisol looks down at the plate number inked on her wrist, faded to a bruise-blue smear but legible. She starts the engine.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the radio)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Then let’s do arithmetic, tío.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. OLD RANCH ROAD - WATER DEPOT - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Tanker trucks nose up to an unmarked standpipe like calves to a trough. From the ditch, Marisol films on her phone. A hand lands on her shoulder — CHUY. She freezes. He looks at the trucks, then at her. He angles his flashlight down, giving her the dark.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHUY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(low)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My brother farms off Canal Siete. Film the plates.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She films. In frame: a tanker’s plate — RV-04-771 — the number on her wrist. The standpipe valve opens with a groan and somewhere under their feet the aquifer leaves home.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(whispering)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Where does it cross? There’s a buyer. Anexo C has the buyer.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHUY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Friday. The trust tankers roll the same night these do. One kind of truck for the radio. One kind for the border. Same water. He is generous with what is not his.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Chuy reaches into his jacket. A folded paper, soft from being carried a long time. He doesn’t hand it over. Not yet.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>CHUY</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>He trusted me to burn the office copies in March. I burned paper. Not this paper. You walk it in front of everyone, señora — or you give it back and we never stood here.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She takes it. ANEXO C. Volumes. Price per cubic meter. And a buyer’s signature line she reads twice.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. TOWN HALL - EJIDO ASSEMBLY - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Packed hall. Raúl at the microphone, mid-reassurance. Marisol walks the center aisle and lays the ledger — flour dust and all — on the table, her phone on top, video queued.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>My father counted every liter you stole. I finished his math.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The hall erupts. Raúl reaches for the ledger. A dozen phones rise, recording. He stops.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAÚL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(to the hall, smiling)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Friends. Grief is standing at a microphone. What you are looking at is a sad woman with her father’s scribbles. San Blas knows me. Sixty years, San Blas knows this family.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Marisol presses play. The speakers carry the depot: water roaring into tankers in the dark, the plate RV-04-771 filling the projection wall. The roar of the hall dies to nothing. Just the recorded water, pouring and pouring.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Anexo C. Forty thousand cubic meters a season, priced per cubic meter, signed for the buyer with your own pen, tío. The trust tankers and the border tankers drink from the same pipe. San Blas is the pipe.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She unfolds Chuy’s paper and lays it beside the ledger. The EJIDO PRESIDENT looks at Raúl. Raúl looks at the paper. For the first time all night, he has no arithmetic. The president reaches for the gavel like a man reaching for high ground.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition"><Text>CUT TO:</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. REYES FAMILY HOUSE - PORCH - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Later. The house behind them finally quiet. Lupita’s phone will not stop buzzing on the step like a beetle on its back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Forty thousand views, tía. A reporter from the capital wants the video. Two reporters. Ceci says we’re trending in Hermosillo.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Views don’t refill an aquifer.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>No. But now everybody watches the water. Todos, tía. That’s what your numbers needed. Witnesses.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Far off on the highway, a truck engine. They both look up, trained now. It passes, ordinary, headed somewhere with nothing to hide. They breathe. The crickets take the night back.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>EXT. REYES FAMILY HOUSE - DAWN</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>First light. Marisol pries the boards off her father’s office window. Inside: his desk, his chair, his adding machine. Lupita appears with two cups of coffee, hands her one.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>LUPITA</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>So you’re staying.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARISOL</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(looking at the desk)</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Somebody has to keep score.</Text></Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She sets the ledger on the desk and opens it to the first blank page her father never filled. Uncaps his pen. Lupita drags a second chair to the desk’s far side and sits like she means to be there a long time. Beyond the window, the sun clears the ridge and lays a stripe of light across the empty column where the water goes next.</Text></Paragraph>
  </Content>
</FinalDraft>
